text:
“Thank you,’ said Maggie, looking at the food without taking it;
“but will you give me some bread-and-butter and tea instead? I
don’t like bacon.”

“Oh, I know what you’ve been doing,” said Tom;
“you’ve been reading the English at the end. Any donkey can do
that.”

“Tom, I  will not  bear it, I  will  scream,” said
Maggie, at the first movement of the sword. “You’ll hurt yourself;
you’ll cut your head off!”

“Oh, yes, better,” she answered immediately. “No, not better;
because I don’t think I  could  love you better than Tom. But I
should be so sorry,— so sorry  for you.”

“No, he didn’t say that. He didn’t quite promise me anything;
he seemed to think I couldn’t have a very good situation. I’m too
young.”

“Let me put the sovereigns in the bag again,” said Maggie;
“and you’ll come and see us when you’ve bought your pack,
Bob.”

“You must not be surprised to see the room look very bare downstairs,
father,” said Maggie; “but there’s your chair and the bureau;
 they’re  not gone.”

“Ah, a deal better—a deal better,” said Mr Tulliver;
“but a hundred and fifty’s an uncommon price. I never thought
o’ paying so much as that.”

“But not for me, not for me,” said Maggie, walking more hurriedly;
“because I should want too much. I must wait; this life will not last
long.”

“Take back your  Corinne ,” said Maggie, drawing a book from
under her shawl. “You were right in telling me she would do me no good;
but you were wrong in thinking I should wish to be like her.”

“Miss Lucy’s called the bell o’ St Ogg’s, they say;
that’s a cur’ous word,” observed Mr Pullet, on whom the
mysteries of etymology sometimes fell with an oppressive weight.

“I don’t care,” said Mr Tulliver, in a thick, fierce voice;
“go and show your back, and tell ’em I thrashed you. Tell ’em
I’ve made things a bit more even i’ the world.”

“Well, then, Dr Kenn has been preaching against buckram, and you ladies
have all been sending him a roundrobin, saying, ‘This is a hard doctrine;
who can bear it?’”

“Maggie’s arms  are  a pretty shape,” said Mrs
Tulliver. “They’re like mine used to be, only mine was never brown;
I wish she’d had  our  family skin.”

“But, papa, you always say Mr Wakem has brought Philip up like a girl;
how came you to think you should get any business knowledge out of him? Those
abrupt questions sounded rather oddly. Philip thought them queer.”

“ I  don’t care about a flood comin’,” said Bob;
“I don’t mind the water, no more nor the land. I’d swim,
 I  would.”

“But lawyer Wakem’s son’s got a hump-back,” said Mrs
Pullet, who felt as if the whole business had a funereal aspect;
“it’s more nat’ral to send  him  to a clergyman.”

“Ay, ay, Gritty,” said the miller, with a new softness in his tone;
“but I’ve allays done what I could for you,” he added, as if
vindicating himself from a reproach.

“It’s boiled hard, brother, and coloured with thrums, very pretty;
it was done o’ purpose for Maggie. Will you please to carry it in your
pocket?”

“Don’t laugh at me, Tom!” she burst out angrily;
“I’m not a stupid. I know a great many things you
don’t.”
